title: You had to fight
new-page: true

You got up. You tried to run, but the water weighed you down, you felt so heavy, Erika shouted “Karl, damn it, stay safe!” You shouted back “Go the other way!” She didn’t reply, you walked on, you had to pick.

It wasn’t much of a choice, you’d have to leave the Pretzel man to his fate, a woman had to be protected, you imagined if it was Erika. Your fists were clenched with rage, if it was Erika, you’d kill whatever scumbag laid a hand on her. 

The policemen saw you approach, but it wasn’t just you, there was other men, even boys, and truly boys, some young, you recognised a few of them from work, or you thought you did, it was hard to tell in the confusion.

Confusion, that’s the word. The policemen, where their faces were blank? Maybe, maybe they weren’t, the water in your eyes, the contempt in your blood was paramount 

One of them, a young man, he had a moustache, raised his truncheon, “Zurück! Zurück!” His voice cracked on the second word, but none of you did, you especially. 
  
There was a boy beside you, when had he appeared? He was...no older than fourteen, his cap was pulled low, but his fists were clenched like a man. You recognised him...
  
Wait, from where? The factory floor? Tenement stairs- you couldn't place it, there wasn’t time. His mouth was open, shouting something you couldn’t hear over whistles,screams, wet slab of boots on cobble

The first blow came from nowhere, a truncheon to your shoulder, a crack, it was boiling in your arm. You stumbled, caught yourself on a comrade’s back and pushed forward. 
  
The women, she had groceries, still, she was there, on her knees, a policeman standing over her. He was about to bring his nightstick on her skull. 
  
You didn’t think you just lunged, you lunged with your energy pushing against the weight. Your fist connected with his jaw, this was a punch you’d never thrown in your life.
  
His head snapped back, the truncheon clattered on the ground. For half a second, you felt like a God

Then the others came to you. A blow to ribs folded you in half, another to the back of your head spent the world in a spin. You were on the ground, you didn’t recall falling, the cobblestones were cold against your cheek, wet with the water, wet now with something warmer. 
  
Blood? Yours or his? Someone leses? You couldn’t tell, you couldn’t feel anything. Get up, you told yourself, get up you fool, Erika is-!
  
